### READING (BlackThrush, 2026-08-23) — the row, with both statistics and the caveat that binds

## THE ROW

    competitive_ratio  0.713729  ci95 [0.702916, 0.722349]   AFTER  (b923646ab, 12.010 Ir/elem)
    competitive_ratio  0.663058  ci95 [0.657063, 0.678674]   BEFORE (8e6816aa5, 15.000 Ir/elem)

The CIs are DISJOINT — the baseline's upper edge 0.678674 sits below this run's lower
edge 0.702916, a gap of +0.024242. On the harness's own statistic the ratio improved
+7.64%.

STILL A LOSS AGAINST THE INCUMBENT: 0.714 < 1.0, `competitive_claim=FAIL`. We remain
about 1.40x slower than SuperLU on this cell. What moved is the size of the deficit.

## THE NULLS ARE CLEAN THIS TIME — and that is the diagnostic doing its job twice

    all_null_ci95_span_unity = TRUE      (it was FALSE on the contaminated run)
    candidate_A/A            0.980381  [0.931770, 1.013592]   spans unity
    control_A/A              0.989593  [0.950090, 1.011669]   spans unity
    candidate_live_pair_A/A  1.022200  [0.996092, 1.064472]   spans unity
    live_A/A                 0.993894  [0.981199, 1.004094]   spans unity

`null_medians_within_2pct=false` still, because two medians sit 2.0-2.2% off unity —
but every CI spans 1.0, so this is IMPRECISION, not the arm-order bias that voided the
previous attempt. That is exactly the distinction `all_null_ci95_span_unity` was added
to draw, and it has now separated a void run from a usable one.

## TWO STATISTICS DISAGREE ON THE MAGNITUDE, and I am not picking the flattering one

                          baseline      after      change
    candidate p50 (ms)   18.278542   17.540564    -4.04%
    live scipy p50 (ms)  11.980163   11.698059    -2.35%   <- host control
    median-of-ratios       0.663058    0.713729    +7.64%   <- harness statistic, CIs disjoint
    ratio-of-medians       0.655422    0.666915    +1.75%   <- cruder view

The harness's paired statistic (median over per-round live/candidate ratios) says
+7.64%. The ratio of the two medians says +1.75%. Both are positive, so the DIRECTION
is not in doubt, but a 4x spread in magnitude between two summaries of the same data
means the per-round pairing is skewed and the +7.64% should not be quoted as the effect
size on its own.

The raw arms are the sober view: we got 4.04% faster while the host — measured by
SciPy's own arm, which is the control for host state — got 2.35% faster. That leaves
roughly 1.7% attributable, which lands inside the "plausibly a few percent" band I
registered BEFORE this run, and is what the counted bound predicts: the merge inner
loop is part of the factor, the factor is about two thirds of a factor-plus-16-solves
job, so a 19.9% cut in that loop's marginal cost cannot be worth 19.9% of the job.

## THE CAVEAT THAT BINDS: this is an ACROSS-RUN comparison

The four A/A nulls certify each run internally. NOTHING spans the two runs — they are
separate invocations, and the load gate never opened (requested 1-min < 15, polled the
full 20 minutes, `window_opened=0`, loadavg 22.15 at start against ~20 for the
baseline). Comparing across runs with no null covering the comparison is the same
defect that made the eigh crossover claim collapse when its n=512 ordering flipped
between two runs of the same ELF.

It is a weaker design than a paired one, and the honest reason I could not do better is
that the OLD kernel is not behind a toggle — both arms cannot live in one binary the way
`MWU_FORCE_TIE_RESORT` lets the mannwhitneyu arms. What would settle it: put the
pre-lever slice form behind a `PerfToggle` and alternate the two arms ABBA inside one
window, position-balanced, with its own A/A null.

## VERDICT

COUNTED: 15.000 -> 12.010 Ir per element-update, deterministic, the bar cleared. That is
the evidence this lever rests on.
END-TO-END: consistent with the count and CI-disjoint on the harness statistic, but
across-run, magnitude-ambiguous between two summaries, and taken outside the requested
load gate. Suggestive, not certified.

